======================================================================================================================
B.  THE ATTACK: how much of the +0.667 belongs to the FRAMEWORK, and how much to the stellar-mass ordering?
======================================================================================================================
  On the isolated branch (6 of 8 objects) R_half cancels identically out of log10(g_obs/g_iso):
      log10( 3 sigma^2 / R ) - log10( sqrt(G M a0) / R )  =  2 log sigma - 0.5 log M + const.
  So the 'framework residual' is the deep-MOND M-sigma relation. Compare it against theory-free orderings.

  ordering correlated against the SAME beta_z                   rho   p_exact   sigma
  g03 framework residual   log10(g_obs/g_pred)              +0.6667    0.0831    1.73
  deep-MOND proxy          2 log sigma - 0.5 log M*         +0.6667    0.0831    1.73
  MINUS stellar mass       -log M*   (NO physics at all)    +0.6826    0.0729    1.79
  dispersion alone         log sigma                        +0.2530    0.5419    0.61
  size alone               -log R_half                      +0.2143    0.6191    0.50
  Newtonian mutation       log10(g_obs/g_N)                 +0.4286    0.2992    1.04
  [FAIL] V5 (IS THE RESIDUAL MORE THAN A MASS ORDERING?) the framework's residual must correlate with the published anisotropies MORE STRONGLY than a theory-free ordering by stellar mass alone. If minus-log-M* -- which uses no gravity, no kernel, no a0, no dispersion and no radius -- matches or beats the residual, then the quoted correlation carries no information about the framework and none about trajectories   (framework residual rho = +0.6667 (exact p = 0.0831, 1.73 sigma); ordering by -log M* ALONE gives rho = +0.6826 (exact p = 0.0729, 1.79 sigma). Stellar mass on its own reproduces AND EXCEEDS the whole signal. Whatever the correlation is, it is not evidence about a trajectory-dependent modification: it is the statement that fainter classical dwarfs carry higher published beta_z, which dark matter, feedback, or a Hayashi-side sample-size systematic produce as readily)
  [FAIL] V6 (RANK-EQUIVALENCE) if the residual's rank order is IDENTICAL to a theory-free proxy's, then rho cannot distinguish the framework from that proxy, and quoting rho as 'the framework's residual' overstates it   (spearman(residual, 2 log sigma - 0.5 log M*) = +1.0000 -- EXACTLY unity, on both footings. The rank statistic quoted in the claim is numerically identical (+0.6667) whether one uses the framework's kernel or a two-line algebraic proxy with a0 divided out. The kernel, the external-field branch and the value of a0 are all invisible to this estimator)

======================================================================================================================
C.  g03's HEADLINE DEFENCE: 'controlling for stellar mass does NOT weaken it (+0.717)'
======================================================================================================================
  r(residual, beta) = +0.6666   r(residual, logM*) = -0.9225   r(beta, logM*) = -0.4554
  partial NUMERATOR (shared variation left after mass) = +0.2465  <-- FELL from +0.6666, 63 per cent removed
  partial DENOMINATOR                                  = 0.3437   <-- inflates by 2.91x, driven entirely by r(residual,logM*)^2 = 0.851
  Pearson partial = +0.7172  (g03 quotes +0.717); RANK partial = +0.0879
  [FAIL] V7 (THE PARTIAL IS NOT EVIDENCE) g03's verdict states 'Controlling for stellar mass does NOT weaken it (+0.717), so it is not obviously the mass trend dark matter also predicts.' For that to be evidence, the shared variation between residual and beta must SURVIVE removing mass, not merely be renormalised by a collapsing denominator   (the numerator collapses from +0.6666 to +0.2465 -- 63 per cent of the residual-beta covariance IS the mass trend. The partial rises to +0.7172 only because the residual is 85 per cent collinear with log M*, which deflates the denominator 2.91-fold. This is textbook variance deflation, not a surviving signal, and the sentence in g03's verdict is wrong)
  [FAIL] V8 (WRONG CURRENCY) the mass-controlled statistic must be reported in the SAME currency as the headline. The claim is quoted as a Spearman rho, so the mass control must be a RANK partial; g03 reports only the Pearson partial, which is not the statistic the claim is made in   (rank partial(residual, beta | log M*) = +0.0879 on the f09 table, against the Pearson partial +0.7172 that g03 quotes. In the currency the claim is actually stated in, conditioning on stellar-mass rank leaves essentially nothing. g03 computes no rank partial anywhere)

